<refentry id="cypher-createvlabel">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-createvlabel">
  <primary>CREATE VLABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE VLABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE VLABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>create a vertex label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE [ UNLOGGED ] VLABEL [ IF NOT EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>
    [ ( <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable> [, ...] ) ]
    [ DISABLE INDEX ]
    [ INHERITS ( <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable> [, ...] ) ]
    [ WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">storage_parameter</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ...] ) ]
    [ TABLESPACE <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable> ]

<phrase>where <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable> is one of:</phrase>

    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ( <replaceable class="parameter">property_key</replaceable> )
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ALWAYS AS ( <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ) [ STORED ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createvlabel-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   A vertex label is a table with an <structfield>id</structfield> of type <type>graphid</type>
   and a <structfield>properties</structfield> column of type <type>jsonb</type>.
   <command>CREATE VLABEL</command> creates that table, the sequence that numbers its ids, and
   the primary key on <structfield>id</structfield>. The label inherits the graph's
   <literal>ag_vertex</literal> unless a parent is named. It is created in the graph named by
   <varname>graph_path</varname>, and the name cannot carry the graph.
  </para>

  <para>
   The options are position sensitive: <literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal>, then
   <literal>INHERITS</literal>, then <literal>WITH</literal>, then
   <literal>TABLESPACE</literal>. Any other order is a syntax error. Cypher also creates a label
   on demand, so <literal>CREATE (:film)</literal> against a graph with no
   <literal>film</literal> label creates one. Use <command>CREATE VLABEL</command> when the
   label needs options, promoted columns or a parent.
  </para>

  <para>
   A promoted property is a real typed column beside the property map, computed from the map
   and stored. Reads of that property are answered from the column instead of walking the JSON
   document, and the column can carry an index over the value's own type. The three forms
   differ only in where the value comes from. <literal>year int GENERATED</literal> mirrors the
   property whose key is the column name. <literal>released text GENERATED
   (releaseDate)</literal> mirrors the named key, so the column may be called something else.
   The long form computes the column from a SQL expression over the label's own columns.
  </para>

  <para>
   The property key comes from the generation expression, not from the column name, and is
   recorded in <structname>ag_label_property</structname>. A query therefore keeps naming the
   property, and the column name never appears in Cypher. Only a plain read of one key is
   recorded, such as <literal>properties -&gt;&gt; 'year'</literal> with or without a cast.
   Wrap that read in anything else and the result is an ordinary generated column: SQL reads
   it, and <literal>n.<replaceable class="parameter">key</replaceable></literal> does not.
  </para>

  <para>
   Five things a promoted property cannot be. It cannot be left ungenerated, so a bare
   <literal><replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable
   class="parameter">data_type</replaceable></literal> is refused. It cannot be
   <literal>VIRTUAL</literal>, because a virtual generated column cannot carry an index;
   <literal>STORED</literal> is the default and may be left out. It cannot be
   <literal>GENERATED BY DEFAULT</literal>. It cannot be named
   <structfield>id</structfield>, <structfield>properties</structfield>,
   <structfield>start</structfield> or <structfield>end</structfield>, the structural columns
   of a graph element. And it cannot carry a collation of its own: the property map compares
   strings with the database default, so a column collated otherwise would order and compare
   the same values differently, and which of the two answered a read would decide the result
   rather than the speed. That holds however the collation arrives &mdash; written down, brought
   by a domain, or given later by <link linkend="sql-altertable"><command>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER
   COLUMN ... TYPE</command></link>. A key an ancestor label already promotes is refused too,
   unless the column name is the same one.
  </para>

  <para>
   The data type has to have an immutable text input function and cannot fix a length.
   <type>date</type>, <type>timestamp</type>, <type>timestamptz</type> and array types are
   refused with <literal>generation expression is not immutable</literal>, and
   <type>varchar(n)</type> and <type>char(n)</type> because fitting a value to a length would
   let the column and the property hold different values. Accepted are <type>smallint</type>,
   <type>integer</type>, <type>bigint</type>, <type>numeric</type>, <type>real</type>,
   <type>double precision</type>, <type>text</type>, <type>boolean</type>, <type>uuid</type>
   and <type>jsonb</type>, and a type an extension provides such as <type>vector</type>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createvlabel-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>UNLOGGED</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Create the label's table as unlogged. Its contents are not written to the write ahead log
      and are lost after a crash.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error when a label of that name already exists. Nothing is
      created, and the existing label's options are left as they are.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the label to create, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A property to mirror into a typed column, in one of the three forms above. Columns are
      added after <structfield>properties</structfield>, in the order written.
      <replaceable class="parameter">property_key</replaceable> is an identifier and is folded
      to lower case, matching the way Cypher stores map keys.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Create the label's indexes but leave them invalid, so that loading rows does not maintain
      them. Reads and writes still work and the planner ignores an invalid index. Rebuild with
      <xref linkend="cypher-reindex"/>. There is no <literal>ENABLE INDEX</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>INHERITS ( <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable> [, ...] )</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Parent labels, replacing the default parent <literal>ag_vertex</literal>. Each has to be
      a vertex label in the same graph.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">storage_parameter</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ...] )</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Storage parameters for the label's table, as for <command>CREATE TABLE</command>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>TABLESPACE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Tablespace for the label's table. Without it, <varname>default_tablespace</varname> decides.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createvlabel-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build a graph of their own and drop it at the end:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH vlabeldemo;
SET graph_path = vlabeldemo;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE VLABEL IF NOT EXISTS film;

NOTICE:  label "film" already exists, skipping
CREATE VLABEL
</programlisting>
   A label created under a parent is matched by a pattern on the parent, and
   <function>label()</function> still reports the label the vertex was created with:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL work;
CREATE VLABEL documentary INHERITS (work);
CREATE (:documentary {title: 'Free Solo'});
MATCH (n:work) RETURN label(n) AS label, n.title AS title;

     label     |    title
---------------+-------------
 "documentary" | "Free Solo"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Promote two properties to typed columns. Writing does not change: the value still goes into
   the property map and the column follows it. The read is answered from the column, so it
   arrives as the column's type rather than as <type>jsonb</type>:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL title (year int GENERATED, name text GENERATED);
CREATE (:title {name: 'Arrival', year: 2016});
MATCH (t:title) RETURN t.name AS name, t.year AS year;

  name   | year
---------+------
 Arrival | 2016
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   A value the column cannot hold is refused at write time, naming both sides:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:title {name: 42, year: 2016});

ERROR:  property "name" holds a number
DETAIL:  A column promoting this property holds strings.
CONTEXT:  while computing the promoted typed columns of graph label "title"
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The parenthesized form names the key, so the column may be called something else. The key
   was written <literal>releaseDate</literal> and recorded as <literal>releasedate</literal>.
   Cypher names the property, never the column:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL review (released text GENERATED (releaseDate));
CREATE (:review {releaseDate: '2016-11-11'});
MATCH (r:review) RETURN r.releasedate AS released;

  released
------------
 2016-11-11
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   SQL reads the same value as <literal>SELECT released FROM vlabeldemo.review</literal>. The
   long form, by contrast, is a promotion only when its expression is a plain read of one key.
   <literal>handle text GENERATED ALWAYS AS (lower(properties -&gt;&gt; 'name')) STORED</literal>
   computes a column SQL can read, but <literal>s.handle</literal> looks for a key
   <literal>handle</literal> that nothing ever wrote, and finds nothing.
  </para>

  <para>
   Each of these is refused outright:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL bad1 (name text);

ERROR:  a non-generated promoted column is not supported yet
HINT:  Use GENERATED to mirror the property from the jsonb bag.
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL bad2 (name text GENERATED ALWAYS AS (properties -&gt;&gt; 'name') VIRTUAL);

ERROR:  promoted generated properties must be STORED
LINE 1: ...e text GENERATED ALWAYS AS (properties -&gt;&gt; 'name') VIRTUAL);
                                                              ^
HINT:  A VIRTUAL generated column cannot carry an index.
</programlisting>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH vlabeldemo CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createvlabel-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <application>pg_dump</application> writes a label with an explicit label id, as
   <literal>CREATE VLABEL ONLY <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> (
   <replaceable class="parameter">label_id</replaceable> )</literal>, so that a restore keeps
   the ids a dumped graph handed out. That form creates no parent, no id default and no
   primary key. Write it only when reproducing a dump.
  </para>

  <para>
   <varname>enable_property_promotion</varname> decides whether a Cypher property read is
   answered from a promoted column. It is on by default, and turning it off can change results
   as well as plans, because a column compares and orders values with its own type's rules
   while the property map uses <type>jsonb</type> ordering. See
   <xref linkend="agensgraph-config"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createvlabel-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no label DDL. Its schema mechanism is the graph type of clause
   12.6, which declares node types and edge types for a whole graph at once. AgensGraph
   declares labels one at a time, and its graphs are open: a label puts no restriction on
   which properties its vertices carry.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE VLABEL</command> corresponds to <command>CREATE TABLE</command>.
   <literal>UNLOGGED</literal>, <literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal>, <literal>INHERITS</literal>,
   <literal>WITH</literal> and <literal>TABLESPACE</literal> mean there what they mean here,
   and each promoted property becomes a <literal>GENERATED ALWAYS AS ... STORED</literal>
   column. The rest has no counterpart: a fixed column list, a registration in
   <structname>ag_label</structname> and <structname>ag_label_property</structname>, an
   automatic parent, and a fixed option order. Going the other way, a label's table cannot be
   reshaped with <command>ALTER TABLE</command> unless <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is
   on.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

